{"task_id": "HumanEval/53", "solution": "# file: calculator/calculator.py\nfrom __future__ import annotations\nfrom typing import Union\n\nNumber = Union[int, float]\n\n\nclass Calculator:\n    \"\"\"\n    A stateless calculator providing a small set of basic arithmetic.\n\n    The implementation is intentionally tiny: it only performs addition.\n    The class is designed to be extensible *without* breaking the public\n    contract: new operations can be added as new ``@staticmethod``s.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def add(a: Number, b: Number) -> Number:\n        \"\"\"Return the sum of *a* and *b*.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(a, (int, float)):\n            raise TypeError(f\"Operand a must be int or float, got {type(a)!r}\")\n        if not isinstance(b, (int, float)):\n            raise TypeError(f\"Operand b must be int or float, got {type(b)!r}\")\n        return a + b\n\n\n# file: calculator/__init__.py\n# Expose public interface\nfrom .calculator import Calculator\n\n__all__ = [\"Calculator\"]\n\n\n# file: calculator/_version.py\n__version__ = \"0.1.0\"\n\n\n# file: pyproject.toml\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"setuptools>=61.0\", \"wheel\"]\nbuild-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\"\n\n[tool.setuptools.packages.find]\nwhere = [\".\"]\n\ninclude = [\"calculator*\"]\n\n[tool.flake8]\nmax-line-length = 88\nextend-ignore = [\"E203\"]\nselect = [\"E\", \"W\", \"F\"]\n\n[tool.mypy]\nstrict = true\nignore_missing_imports = true\n\n\n# file: setup.cfg\n\n[metadata]\nname = simple-add\nversion = attr: calculator._version.__version__\nauthor = Your Name\nauthor_email = your.email@example.com\ndescription = A lightweight Python package for addition\nlong_description = file: README.md\nlong_description_content_type = text/markdown\nurl = https://github.com/yourusername/simple-add\nclassifiers =\n    Programming Language :: Python :: 3\n    License :: OSI Approved :: MIT License\n    Operating System :: OS Independent\n    Development Status :: 3 - Alpha\n    Intended Audience :: Developers\n    Topic :: Software Development :: Libraries :: Python Modules\n    Topic :: Scientific/Engineering :: Mathematics\n\n[options]\npackages = find:\ninclude_package_data = true\npython_requires = >=3.8\ninstall_requires =\n\n[options.packages.find]\nwhere = .\ninclude = calculator*\n\n[options.extras_require]\ndev =\n    pytest>=7.0\n    flake8>=4.0\n    mypy>=0.900\n    build>=0.7.0\n    twine>=3.4\n\n\n# file: README.md\n# simple-add\n\nA lightweight Python package exposing a single arithmetic operation: addition. This package serves as a clear example of clean architecture, packaging, testing, and type-checking practices.\n\n## Installation\n\n```bash\n# Recommended for development, installs dev dependencies\npip install -e .[dev]\n```\n\n## Usage\n\nThe package exposes a single public API: `Calculator.add`.\n\n```python\nfrom calculator import Calculator\n\n# Add two integers\nresult_int = Calculator.add(5, 3)\nprint(f\"5 + 3 = {result_int}\")\n\n# Add two floats\nresult_float = Calculator.add(1.5, 2.5)\nprint(f\"1.5 + 2.5 = {result_float}\")\n\n# Add with negatives\nresult_neg = Calculator.add(-10, 7)\nprint(f\"-10 + 7 = {result_neg}\")\n\n# Type validation (will raise TypeError)\ntry:\n    Calculator.add(\"hello\", 1)\nexcept TypeError as e:\n    print(f\"Error: {e}\")\n```\n\n## Development\n\n### Setup\n\nClone the repository and install development dependencies:\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/yourusername/simple-add.git # Replace with actual repo\ncd simple-add\npip install -e .[dev]\n```\n\n### Testing\n\nRun the test suite using `pytest`:\n\n```bash\npytest\n```\n\n*(Note: As per specific instruction, tests are not included in the generated code output, but this section remains for documentation.)*\n\n### Linting & Type-Checking\n\nEnsure code quality with `flake8` and static type-checking with `mypy`:\n\n```bash\nflake8\nmypy --strict\n```\n\n### Building Distribution\n\nTo build the distributable wheel and source tarball:\n\n```bash\npython -m build\n```\n\nThe distribution files will be generated in the `dist/` directory.\n\n## Extending the Calculator\n\nThe `Calculator` class is designed to be extensible. New arithmetic operations can be added as static methods without breaking existing callers. For example:\n\n```python\n# In calculator/calculator.py\nclass Calculator:\n    # ... existing add method ...\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def subtract(a: Number, b: Number) -> Number:\n        \"\"\"Return the difference of *a* and *b*.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(a, (int, float)):\n            raise TypeError(f\"Operand a must be int or float, got {type(a)!r}\")\n        if not isinstance(b, (int, float)):\n            raise TypeError(f\"Operand b must be int or float, got {type(b)!r}\")\n        return a - b\n```\n\nThis design ensures future features can be integrated seamlessly."}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/54", "solution": "def same_chars(s0: str, s1: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Check if two words have the same characters.\n\n    The comparison ignores the frequency of each character and\n    focuses solely on the presence of unique characters in each\n    string.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    s0 : str\n        First input string.\n    s1 : str\n        Second input string.\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    bool\n        True if both strings contain exactly the same set of\n        unique characters, otherwise False.\n\n    Raises\n    ------\n    TypeError\n        If either argument is not a string.\n\n    Examples\n    --------\n    >>> same_chars('eabcdzzzz', 'dddzzzzzzzddeddabc')\n    True\n    >>> same_chars('abcd', 'dddddddabc')\n    True\n    >>> same_chars('dddddddabc', 'abcd')\n    True\n    >>> same_chars('eabcd', 'dddddddabc')\n    False\n    >>> same_chars('abcd', 'dddddddabce')\n    False\n    >>> same_chars('eabcdzzzz', 'dddzzzzzzzddddabc')\n    False\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(s0, str) or not isinstance(s1, str):\n        raise TypeError(\"Both inputs must be strings\")\n\n    return set(s0) == set(s1)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    import doctest\n    doctest.testmod()\n    import unittest\n\n    class TestSameChars(unittest.TestCase):\n        def test_same_sets(self):\n            self.assertTrue(same_chars(\"abc\", \"cba\"))\n            self.assertTrue(same_chars(\"\", \"\"))\n            self.assertTrue(same_chars(\"aabbcc\", \"abc\"))\n\n        def test_different_sets(self):\n            self.assertFalse(same_chars(\"abc\", \"abcd\"))\n            self.assertFalse(same_chars(\"xyz\", \"xy\"))\n            self.assertFalse(same_chars(\"abcd\", \"efgh\"))\n\n        def test_error_handling(self):\n            with self.assertRaises(TypeError):\n                same_chars(None, \"abc\")\n            with self.assertRaises(TypeError):\n                same_chars(\"abc\", 123)\n\n    unittest.main(argv=['first-arg-is-ignored'], exit=False)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/55", "solution": "def fib(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Return n-th Fibonacci number with 0-indexed definition.\n    \n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    n : int\n        The index of the Fibonacci sequence (non-negative integer).\n        \n    Returns\n    -------\n    int\n        The Fibonacci number at position n.\n        \n    Raises\n    ------\n    ValueError\n        If n is negative.\n        \n    Examples\n    --------\n    >>> fib(10)\n    55\n    >>> fib(1)\n    1\n    >>> fib(8)\n    21\n    >>> fib(0)\n    0\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be a non-negative integer\")\n    \n    a, b = 0, 1\n    for _ in range(n):\n        a, b = b, a + b\n    return a"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/56", "solution": "def correct_bracketing(brackets: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Check whether a string consisting solely of '<' and '>' is correctly bracketed.\n    A string is correctly bracketed if:\n        * Every '<' has a corresponding '>' after it.\n        * No '>' occurs before its matching '<'.\n    This is equivalent to a well-formed sequence of brackets.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    brackets : str\n        A string containing only the characters '<' and '>'.\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    bool\n        True if every opening bracket has a corresponding closing bracket; False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    balance = 0\n    for ch in brackets:\n        if ch == '<':\n            balance += 1\n        elif ch == '>':\n            balance -= 1\n        else:\n            # Unexpected character \u2013 the string must contain only '<' and '>'.\n            return False\n\n        if balance < 0:\n            # A closing bracket appeared before its matching opening bracket.\n            return False\n\n    return balance == 0  # True if all opened brackets were closed\n\n# Examples for doctest:\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    import doctest\n    doctest.testmod()\n    # Additional tests\n    assert not correct_bracketing(\"<\")\n    assert correct_bracketing(\"<>\")\n    assert correct_bracketing(\"<<><>>\")\n    assert not correct_bracketing(\"><<>\")"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/57", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Sequence\n\ndef monotonic(l: Sequence[int] | Sequence[float]) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Return True if the sequence elements are monotonically increasing or decreasing.\n    \n    Examples:\n        >>> monotonic([1, 2, 4, 20])\n        True\n        >>> monotonic([1, 20, 4, 10])\n        False\n        >>> monotonic([4, 1, 0, -10])\n        True\n    \"\"\"\n    it = iter(l)\n    try:\n        prev = next(it)\n    except StopIteration:\n        return True  # empty sequence is considered monotonic\n\n    direction = 0  # 0: undetermined, 1: increasing, -1: decreasing\n    for current in it:\n        diff = current - prev\n        if diff != 0:\n            new_dir = 1 if diff > 0 else -1\n            if direction == 0:\n                direction = new_dir\n            elif new_dir != direction:\n                return False\n        prev = current\n    return True\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/58", "solution": "def common(l1: list, l2: list):\n    \"\"\"Return sorted unique common elements for two lists.\n    >>> common([1, 4, 3, 34, 653, 2, 5], [5, 7, 1, 5, 9, 653, 121])\n    [1, 5, 653]\n    >>> common([5, 3, 2, 8], [3, 2])\n    [2, 3]\n    \"\"\"\n    return sorted(set(l1) & set(l2))"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/59", "solution": "def largest_prime_factor(n: int):\n    \"\"\"Return the largest prime factor of n. Assume n > 1 and is not a prime.\n    >>> largest_prime_factor(13195)\n    29\n    >>> largest_prime_factor(2048)\n    2\n    \"\"\"\n    import math\n    if n <= 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be greater than 1\")\n\n    largest = None\n\n    # Remove factor 2\n    while n % 2 == 0:\n        largest = 2\n        n //= 2\n\n    # Test odd factors\n    factor = 3\n    while factor <= math.isqrt(n):\n        while n % factor == 0:\n            largest = factor\n            n //= factor\n        factor += 2\n\n    # If remaining n is a prime greater than 2\n    if n > 2:\n        largest = n\n\n    return largest\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    import doctest\n    doctest.testmod()"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/60", "solution": "def sum_to_n(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Return the sum of all integers from 1 to ``n`` inclusive.\n\n    The result is computed using the arithmetic series formula::\n\n        result = n * (n + 1) // 2\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    n : int\n        The upper bound of the summation. Must be a non\u2011negative integer.\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    int\n        The sum of the series 1 + 2 + \u2026 + n.\n\n    Raises\n    ------\n    TypeError\n        If ``n`` is not an ``int``.\n    ValueError\n        If ``n`` is negative.\n\n    Examples\n    --------\n    >>> sum_to_n(30)\n    465\n    >>> sum_to_n(100)\n    5050\n    >>> sum_to_n(5)\n    15\n    >>> sum_to_n(10)\n    55\n    >>> sum_to_n(1)\n    1\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"n must be an integer\")\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be non\u2011negative\")\n    return n * (n + 1) // 2\n\n"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/61", "solution": "def correct_bracketing(brackets: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Return True if every opening bracket has a corresponding closing bracket.\n\n    The function processes a string consisting solely of ``'('`` and ``')'`` characters.\n    It verifies that opening brackets are matched by closing brackets in the correct order.\n    An empty string is considered correctly bracketed.\n\n    Args:\n        brackets: A string containing only parenthesis characters ``'('`` and ``')'``.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if the brackets are correctly balanced, otherwise False.\n\n    Complexity:\n        - Time: O(n), where n is the length of ``brackets``.\n        - Space: O(1).\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> correct_bracketing(\"(\")\n        False\n        >>> correct_bracketing(\"()\")\n        True\n        >>> correct_bracketing(\"(()())\")\n        True\n        >>> correct_bracketing(\")(()\")\n        False\n    \"\"\"\n    count = 0\n    for ch in brackets:\n        if ch == '(':\n            count += 1\n        elif ch == ')':\n            count -= 1\n        else:\n            raise ValueError(\"Invalid character in input: only '(' and ')' are allowed\")\n        if count < 0:  # more closing brackets seen than opening ones\n            return False\n    return count == 0\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    import doctest\n    doctest.testmod()\n    import unittest\n\n    class TestCorrectBracketing(unittest.TestCase):\n        def test_examples(self):\n            self.assertFalse(correct_bracketing(\"(\"))\n            self.assertTrue(correct_bracketing(\"()\"))\n            self.assertTrue(correct_bracketing(\"(()())\"))\n            self.assertFalse(correct_bracketing(\")(()\"))\n\n        def test_empty(self):\n            self.assertTrue(correct_bracketing(\"\"))\n\n        def test_invalid(self):\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError):\n                correct_bracketing(\"(a)\")  # contains invalid character\n\n    unittest.main(argv=['first-arg-is-ignored'], exit=False)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/62", "solution": "def derivative(xs: list):\n    \"\"\"xs represent coefficients of a polynomial.\n    xs[0] + xs[1] * x + xs[2] * x^2 + ....\n    Return derivative of this polynomial in the same form.\n    >>> derivative([3, 1, 2, 4, 5])\n    [1, 4, 12, 20]\n    >>> derivative([1, 2, 3])\n    [2, 6]\n    \"\"\"\n    return [coeff * i for i, coeff in enumerate(xs[1:], start=1)] if len(xs) > 1 else []\n"}
